Getting Started.Hello World:
# Hello World

This is the smallest possible ratatui program. It draws one frame with the text
`"Hello World!"` and waits for a keypress before exiting.

```rust
use crossterm::event;

fn main() -> std::io::Result<()> {
    ratatui::run(|terminal| {
        loop {
            terminal.draw(|frame| {
                frame.render_widget("Hello World!", frame.area());
            })?;

            if event::read()?.is_key_press() {
                break Ok(());
            }
        }
    })
}
```

Run it:

```bash
cargo run
```

## What's happening here

- **`ratatui::run(...)`** is the easiest way to start an app. It calls `ratatui::init()`
  before running your closure (which enables raw mode, enters the alternate screen, and
  installs a panic hook that restores the terminal even if your app panics), runs your
  closure with a `&mut DefaultTerminal`, then calls `ratatui::restore()` afterwards to give
  the terminal back to the shell cleanly. This is new as of ratatui 0.30 and is the
  recommended entry point for most apps — see [Terminal Setup](/ratatui/terminal-setup/) for
  what it does under the hood and how to do it manually if you need more control.
- **`terminal.draw(|frame| { ... })`** runs once per frame. Ratatui is an
  **immediate-mode** rendering library: every single frame, your closure must describe the
  *entire* UI from scratch by rendering widgets into the frame. There's no persistent widget
  tree that mutates in place — you rebuild the view every draw call from your app's current
  state. See [Rendering & the Frame Loop](/ratatui/rendering/) for why this design was chosen.
- **`frame.render_widget(widget, area)`** draws a widget into a rectangular `area` of the
  screen. Plain `&str` and `String` implement `Widget` directly, which is why
  `"Hello World!"` can be passed straight in — no need to wrap it in a `Paragraph` for
  something this simple.
- **`frame.area()`** returns a `Rect` covering the whole terminal viewport, so the text is
  drawn starting at the top-left corner.
- **`event::read()`** blocks until a terminal event (keypress, resize, mouse, paste) arrives,
  and **`.is_key_press()`** checks whether it was specifically a key-down event (Crossterm
  fires both press and release events on some terminals, so this avoids exiting twice or on
  a key release).

## The struct-based variant

Real apps usually wrap their state in a struct rather than closing over local variables.
`ratatui::run` works the same way with a method:

```rust
struct App {
    should_quit: bool,
}

impl App {
    fn new() -> Self {
        Self { should_quit: false }
    }

    fn run(mut self, terminal: &mut ratatui::DefaultTerminal) -> std::io::Result<()> {
        while !self.should_quit {
            terminal.draw(|frame| frame.render_widget("Hello World!", frame.area()))?;
            if event::read()?.is_key_press() {
                self.should_quit = true;
            }
        }
        Ok(())
    }
}

fn main() -> std::io::Result<()> {
    ratatui::run(|terminal| App::new().run(terminal))
}
```

This is the shape you'll build on for every real app — see
[Building a Stateful App](/ratatui/building-a-stateful-app/) next.

Getting Started.Terminal Setup:
# Terminal Setup (Manual)

`ratatui::run()` (shown in [Hello World](/ratatui/hello-world/)) handles terminal setup and
teardown for you. This page explains what it does under the hood, for when you need more
control — for example, custom panic handling, a different backend, or an
[inline viewport](/ratatui/inline-viewport/) instead of a full-screen alternate screen.

## What `ratatui::init()` / `ratatui::restore()` do

```rust
fn main() -> std::io::Result<()> {
    let mut terminal = ratatui::init();
    let result = run(&mut terminal);
    ratatui::restore();
    result
}

fn run(terminal: &mut ratatui::DefaultTerminal) -> std::io::Result<()> {
    loop {
        terminal.draw(|frame| frame.render_widget("Hello World!", frame.area()))?;
        if crossterm::event::read()?.is_key_press() {
            break Ok(());
        }
    }
}
```

`ratatui::init()`:
1. Enables **raw mode** — disables line buffering and input echo so every individual
   keypress is delivered to your app instantly instead of waiting for Enter.
2. Enters the **alternate screen** — a separate terminal buffer so your TUI doesn't scroll
   into or overwrite the user's existing shell history; exiting restores exactly what was on
   screen before.
3. Installs a panic hook that restores the terminal (disables raw mode, leaves the alternate
   screen) before printing the panic message, so a crash doesn't leave the user's shell in a
   broken, unreadable state.

`ratatui::restore()` reverses all of that. **Always** make sure this (or your manual
equivalent) runs even on the error path — an app that exits via `std::process::exit` or an
unhandled panic without restoring the terminal will leave the user's shell garbled until they
run `reset` or `stty sane`.

## Fully manual setup (no helper functions)

If you're not using the `DefaultTerminal` alias and want full control over the backend:

```rust
use std::io::stdout;

use crossterm::{
    execute,
    terminal::{EnterAlternateScreen, LeaveAlternateScreen, enable_raw_mode, disable_raw_mode},
};
use ratatui::{Terminal, backend::CrosstermBackend};

fn main() -> std::io::Result<()> {
    enable_raw_mode()?;
    let mut stdout = stdout();
    execute!(stdout, EnterAlternateScreen)?;
    let backend = CrosstermBackend::new(stdout);
    let mut terminal = Terminal::new(backend)?;

    let result = run(&mut terminal);

    disable_raw_mode()?;
    execute!(terminal.backend_mut(), LeaveAlternateScreen)?;
    terminal.show_cursor()?;

    result
}
```

## Panic hooks (color_eyre)

Because a panic mid-draw can leave the terminal in raw/alternate-screen mode, it's common to
pair `ratatui::init()` with [`color_eyre`](https://crates.io/crates/color-eyre) for nicer
error reports that still restore the terminal first:

```bash
cargo add color-eyre
```

```rust
fn main() -> color_eyre::Result<()> {
    color_eyre::install()?;
    let mut terminal = ratatui::init();
    let result = run(&mut terminal);
    ratatui::restore();
    result
}
```

`ratatui::init()` already chains a terminal-restoring panic hook internally, so this combo
gives you both: the terminal is restored on panic, *and* the panic/error is printed nicely
afterward instead of a raw Rust backtrace.

## Inline viewport (not full-screen)

Sometimes you don't want to take over the whole screen — e.g. a build-progress UI that should
scroll normally with the rest of the terminal output. Use `TerminalOptions` with
`Viewport::Inline(height)` instead of the alternate screen:

```rust
use ratatui::{Terminal, TerminalOptions, Viewport, backend::CrosstermBackend};

let backend = CrosstermBackend::new(std::io::stdout());
let mut terminal = Terminal::with_options(
    backend,
    TerminalOptions { viewport: Viewport::Inline(8) },
)?;
```

This renders a fixed-height block of UI inline in the existing scrollback, which then stays
in place as your program prints normal lines above it — useful for progress bars or live
build/test output.

Building an App.The Elm-Style App Loop:
# The Elm-Style App Loop

Most ratatui apps — regardless of size — converge on the same three-part loop:

1. **Render** the UI based on current state.
2. **Read** the next terminal event (keyboard, mouse, resize, paste).
3. **Update** state based on that event, then go back to 1.

This is often called an "Elm-style" architecture (after the Elm language's
model/update/view pattern) because it keeps rendering as a pure function of state, and all
mutation happens in one place in response to events.

```rust
use crossterm::event::{self, Event, KeyCode, KeyEventKind};
use ratatui::{DefaultTerminal, Frame};

#[derive(Default)]
struct App {
    counter: i64,
    should_quit: bool,
}

impl App {
    fn run(mut self, terminal: &mut DefaultTerminal) -> std::io::Result<()> {
        while !self.should_quit {
            terminal.draw(|frame| self.draw(frame))?;
            self.handle_events()?;
        }
        Ok(())
    }

    fn draw(&self, frame: &mut Frame) {
        let text = format!("Counter: {}\n\nUp/Down to change, q to quit", self.counter);
        frame.render_widget(text, frame.area());
    }

    fn handle_events(&mut self) -> std::io::Result<()> {
        // Only reacting to key *press* events avoids double-handling on terminals
        // that also report key release.
        if let Event::Key(key) = event::read()? {
            if key.kind == KeyEventKind::Press {
                match key.code {
                    KeyCode::Char('q') | KeyCode::Esc => self.should_quit = true,
                    KeyCode::Up => self.counter += 1,
                    KeyCode::Down => self.counter -= 1,
                    _ => {}
                }
            }
        }
        Ok(())
    }
}

fn main() -> std::io::Result<()> {
    ratatui::run(|terminal| App::new_default().run(terminal))
}
```

(`App::new_default` above stands in for wherever you construct the initial state —
`App::default()` works fine given the `#[derive(Default)]`.)

## Why `draw` never mutates state

Keep `draw` (or your `ui`/`view` function) read-only. It may be called more than once per
logical update in some setups (e.g. on terminal resize events), and mixing rendering with
mutation makes state changes much harder to reason about and test. All state changes should
happen in the event-handling/update step.

## Non-blocking event polling (for animations, ticks, async work)

`event::read()` blocks forever until an event arrives, which is fine for a static UI but
wrong the moment you want a spinner, a clock, or periodic redraws. Use `event::poll` with a
timeout to build a frame-rate-limited loop instead:

```rust
use std::time::{Duration, Instant};
use crossterm::event::{self, Event};

let tick_rate = Duration::from_millis(50); // ~20 FPS
let mut last_tick = Instant::now();

loop {
    terminal.draw(|frame| app.draw(frame))?;

    let timeout = tick_rate.saturating_sub(last_tick.elapsed());
    if event::poll(timeout)? {
        if let Event::Key(key) = event::read()? {
            app.handle_key(key);
        }
    }

    if last_tick.elapsed() >= tick_rate {
        app.on_tick(); // advance animations, spinners, polling, etc.
        last_tick = Instant::now();
    }

    if app.should_quit {
        break;
    }
}
```

This is the pattern almost every real ratatui app (dashboards, games, spinners) is built on:
`poll` with a short timeout, handle an event if one showed up, otherwise fall through and run
your periodic "tick" logic, then redraw either way.

See [Async Apps with Tokio](/ratatui/async-apps/) for doing the same thing with `tokio`
instead of blocking polling, which is preferable once you're also awaiting network/IO inside
the same app.

Building an App.Handling Input:
# Handling Input

All terminal input arrives through your backend's event stream. With Crossterm:

```rust
use crossterm::event::{self, Event, KeyCode, KeyEvent, KeyEventKind, KeyModifiers};

if let Event::Key(KeyEvent { code, modifiers, kind, .. }) = event::read()? {
    if kind == KeyEventKind::Press {
        match (code, modifiers) {
            (KeyCode::Char('c'), KeyModifiers::CONTROL) => { /* ctrl+c */ }
            (KeyCode::Char('q'), _) => { /* quit */ }
            (KeyCode::Enter, _) => { /* confirm */ }
            (KeyCode::Tab, _) => { /* next field/tab */ }
            (KeyCode::Left | KeyCode::Char('h'), _) => { /* move left, vim-style too */ }
            _ => {}
        }
    }
}
```

## Always check `KeyEventKind::Press`

Some terminals (particularly ones supporting the Kitty keyboard protocol) report both key
*press* and key *release* events. If you don't filter for `Press`, actions can fire twice —
once on press, once on release. This is the single most common bug in first ratatui apps.

## Mouse events

Mouse capture must be explicitly enabled — it's off by default so normal terminal text
selection still works:

```rust
use std::io::stdout;
use crossterm::{execute, event::EnableMouseCapture};

execute!(stdout(), EnableMouseCapture)?;
```

Then handle `Event::Mouse(MouseEvent { kind, column, row, .. })`:

```rust
use crossterm::event::{Event, MouseEventKind};

if let Event::Mouse(mouse) = event::read()? {
    match mouse.kind {
        MouseEventKind::Down(_button) => { /* clicked at mouse.column, mouse.row */ }
        MouseEventKind::ScrollUp => { /* scroll up */ }
        MouseEventKind::ScrollDown => { /* scroll down */ }
        _ => {}
    }
}
```

Remember to `execute!(stdout(), DisableMouseCapture)` on shutdown, symmetrically with
enabling it — `ratatui::restore()` does **not** do this for you automatically since mouse
capture isn't enabled by `ratatui::init()` in the first place.

## Resize events

```rust
use crossterm::event::Event;

if let Event::Resize(width, height) = event::read()? {
    // Usually you don't need to do anything here — the next terminal.draw()
    // call automatically uses the new frame.area(). Useful if you need to
    // react explicitly, e.g. recompute a cache sized to the old dimensions.
}
```

## Paste events (bracketed paste)

Enable bracketed paste to receive multi-character paste events as a single `Event::Paste`
instead of a flood of individual keypresses:

```rust
use crossterm::event::EnableBracketedPaste;
execute!(stdout(), EnableBracketedPaste)?;
```

```rust
if let Event::Paste(text) = event::read()? {
    my_text_input.push_str(&text);
}
```

## Debouncing / non-blocking reads

See [The Elm-Style App Loop](/ratatui/the-elm-style-app-loop/) for using `event::poll(timeout)`
so input handling doesn't block redraws needed for animation or ticking state.

Building an App.Async Apps with Tokio:
# Async Apps with Tokio

Blocking `event::poll`/`event::read` works fine for simple apps, but once you're also
awaiting network requests, file IO, or subprocess output, you'll want an async event loop
instead. This is the standard pattern used by most non-trivial ratatui apps (and by the
`component` template).

```bash
cargo add tokio --features rt-multi-thread,macros
cargo add crossterm --features event-stream
cargo add futures
```

## Reading events as a stream

Crossterm's `event-stream` feature exposes an `EventStream` that implements
`futures::Stream`, so it plugs directly into `tokio::select!`:

```rust
use std::time::Duration;

use crossterm::event::{Event, EventStream, KeyCode};
use futures::{FutureExt, StreamExt};
use tokio::time::interval;

async fn run(mut app: App, terminal: &mut ratatui::DefaultTerminal) -> std::io::Result<()> {
    let mut events = EventStream::new();
    let mut tick = interval(Duration::from_millis(250));

    loop {
        terminal.draw(|frame| app.draw(frame))?;

        tokio::select! {
            maybe_event = events.next().fuse() => {
                if let Some(Ok(Event::Key(key))) = maybe_event {
                    if key.code == KeyCode::Char('q') {
                        break;
                    }
                    app.handle_key(key);
                }
            }
            _ = tick.tick() => {
                app.on_tick(); // periodic work: poll a background task, animate, etc.
            }
        }
    }

    Ok(())
}
```

## Background work via channels

The common shape for "fetch something over the network, then update the UI" is: spawn a
`tokio::task`, have it send results back over an `mpsc` channel, and drain that channel
alongside your event stream in the same `select!`:

```rust
use tokio::sync::mpsc;

enum AppEvent {
    Terminal(crossterm::event::Event),
    DataLoaded(String),
    Tick,
}

let (tx, mut rx) = mpsc::unbounded_channel::<AppEvent>();

// Spawn background work that reports back through the channel:
let tx2 = tx.clone();
tokio::spawn(async move {
    let data = fetch_something().await;
    let _ = tx2.send(AppEvent::DataLoaded(data));
});

// Main loop just drains one channel:
while let Some(event) = rx.recv().await {
    match event {
        AppEvent::Terminal(evt) => { /* handle key/mouse/resize */ }
        AppEvent::DataLoaded(data) => { app.data = Some(data); }
        AppEvent::Tick => { app.on_tick(); }
    }
    terminal.draw(|frame| app.draw(frame))?;
}
```

This "everything becomes one `AppEvent` enum fed through one channel" pattern scales well —
it's the same idea behind the more formal
[Flux/component architectures](/ratatui/application-architecture/), just without the extra
structure until you actually need it.

## `#[tokio::main]`

Wire it up like any other tokio binary:

```rust
#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> color_eyre::Result<()> {
    color_eyre::install()?;
    let mut terminal = ratatui::init();
    let result = run(App::default(), &mut terminal).await;
    ratatui::restore();
    result
}
```

Building an App.Application Architecture:
# Application Architecture

For small apps, a single `App` struct with a `draw` method and a `handle_events` method (see
[The Elm-Style App Loop](/ratatui/the-elm-style-app-loop/)) is all you need. As an app grows
— multiple screens, background tasks, reusable widgets with their own state — three common
patterns show up in the ratatui ecosystem. None of these are enforced by the library itself;
they're just conventions.

## The Elm Architecture

Everything is a pure `(State, Message) -> State` update function, plus a pure
`State -> Ui` view function. All state lives in one place, all mutations happen through
explicit messages/actions rather than scattered `&mut self` calls:

```rust
enum Message {
    Increment,
    Decrement,
    Quit,
}

fn update(state: &mut State, message: Message) {
    match message {
        Message::Increment => state.counter += 1,
        Message::Decrement => state.counter -= 1,
        Message::Quit => state.should_quit = true,
    }
}

fn view(state: &State, frame: &mut ratatui::Frame) {
    frame.render_widget(format!("Counter: {}", state.counter), frame.area());
}
```

Input handling becomes a translation step from raw terminal events into `Message`s, keeping
"what can happen" (the `Message` enum) separate from "how it happens" (key bindings).

## Component Architecture

Each screen/widget is its own struct implementing a shared trait (`draw`, `handle_event`,
`update`), and the top-level `App` just delegates to whichever component is currently active.
This is the pattern behind the official `ratatui/templates` **component** template:

```rust
trait Component {
    fn draw(&mut self, frame: &mut ratatui::Frame, area: ratatui::layout::Rect);
    fn handle_key(&mut self, key: crossterm::event::KeyEvent) -> Option<Action>;
}

struct HomeScreen { /* ... */ }
struct SettingsScreen { /* ... */ }

impl Component for HomeScreen { /* ... */ }
impl Component for SettingsScreen { /* ... */ }

struct App {
    components: Vec<Box<dyn Component>>,
}
```

This scales well once you have several independent screens/panels, each with meaningfully
different state and behavior, and you want to add/remove/reorder them without touching a
giant match statement.

## Flux Architecture

A stricter variant of the Elm pattern with a single, unidirectional data flow: a central
`Store` holds all state, components dispatch `Action`s to it (often over an async channel),
the store updates and emits new state, and views re-render from that state. This is
overkill for most TUIs but can help in larger apps with many independent producers of state
changes (multiple background tasks, multiple input sources) that would otherwise fight over
direct `&mut` access to shared state.

## Picking one

- **Single struct + match statement** — anything under a few hundred lines, single screen.
- **Elm-style Message enum** — once your input-handling `match` gets hard to follow, or you
  want handling logic that's trivially unit-testable without a real terminal.
- **Component trait objects** — multiple distinct screens/panels/widgets, each owning
  meaningfully different state.
- **Flux-style store + channels** — multiple async background producers of state changes
  (several long-running tasks, sockets, timers) that all need to update shared state safely.

Most real-world ratatui apps end up as some hybrid: a Component-style split for screens, with
Elm-style Message enums for each component's internal input handling.

Building an App.Project Structure:
# Project Structure

For anything beyond a single-file toy, split `main.rs` into focused modules. This is the
shape used by the official `ratatui/templates` "simple" template and scales down cleanly for
smaller apps (just merge files back together when a split module gets too small to be worth
its own file).

```
src/
├── main.rs      # entry point: terminal setup/teardown, calls App::run
├── app.rs       # App struct + state + should_quit flag
├── event.rs     # event enum + reading loop (crossterm -> your AppEvent)
├── ui.rs        # draw(app, frame) — pure rendering, no state mutation
├── update.rs    # update(app, event) — all state mutation lives here
└── tui.rs       # thin wrapper around Terminal, entering/leaving alt screen
```

## main.rs

```rust
mod app;
mod event;
mod tui;
mod ui;
mod update;

use app::App;

fn main() -> color_eyre::Result<()> {
    color_eyre::install()?;
    let mut terminal = ratatui::init();
    let result = App::new().run(&mut terminal);
    ratatui::restore();
    result
}
```

## app.rs

```rust
use crate::event::AppEvent;

pub struct App {
    pub counter: i64,
    pub should_quit: bool,
}

impl App {
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        Self { counter: 0, should_quit: false }
    }

    pub fn run(mut self, terminal: &mut ratatui::DefaultTerminal) -> color_eyre::Result<()> {
        while !self.should_quit {
            terminal.draw(|frame| crate::ui::draw(&self, frame))?;
            let event = crate::event::next()?;
            crate::update::update(&mut self, event);
        }
        Ok(())
    }
}
```

## event.rs

```rust
use crossterm::event::{self, Event, KeyCode, KeyEventKind};

pub enum AppEvent {
    Quit,
    Increment,
    Decrement,
    Tick,
    None,
}

pub fn next() -> color_eyre::Result<AppEvent> {
    if event::poll(std::time::Duration::from_millis(100))? {
        if let Event::Key(key) = event::read()? {
            if key.kind == KeyEventKind::Press {
                return Ok(match key.code {
                    KeyCode::Char('q') => AppEvent::Quit,
                    KeyCode::Up => AppEvent::Increment,
                    KeyCode::Down => AppEvent::Decrement,
                    _ => AppEvent::None,
                });
            }
        }
        Ok(AppEvent::None)
    } else {
        Ok(AppEvent::Tick)
    }
}
```

## update.rs

```rust
use crate::app::App;
use crate::event::AppEvent;

pub fn update(app: &mut App, event: AppEvent) {
    match event {
        AppEvent::Quit => app.should_quit = true,
        AppEvent::Increment => app.counter += 1,
        AppEvent::Decrement => app.counter -= 1,
        AppEvent::Tick | AppEvent::None => {}
    }
}
```

## ui.rs

```rust
use ratatui::Frame;
use crate::app::App;

pub fn draw(app: &App, frame: &mut Frame) {
    frame.render_widget(format!("Counter: {}", app.counter), frame.area());
}
```

This gives you a clean separation: `event.rs` translates raw terminal events into your own
`AppEvent` enum, `update.rs` is the only place state is mutated, and `ui.rs` never mutates
anything — exactly the discipline described in
[The Elm-Style App Loop](/ratatui/the-elm-style-app-loop/).

For larger apps with multiple independent screens, see the **Component Architecture**
pattern in [Application Architecture](/ratatui/application-architecture/), which replaces
this flat module split with a `components/` directory of self-contained screen structs.

Layout.Centering & Popups:
# Centering & Popups

A very common need: draw a fixed-size dialog/popup box centered over the rest of the UI (a
confirmation prompt, a help overlay, an "are you sure?" dialog).

## Centering a fixed-size Rect (0.30+ helper methods)

```rust
use ratatui::layout::Constraint;

let popup_area = frame.area().centered(Constraint::Length(40), Constraint::Length(10));
```

## Centering manually (works on any ratatui version)

If you're on an older version, or want to see what the helper does under the hood, centering
is just two nested layout splits — one to carve out the vertical middle, one to carve out the
horizontal middle:

```rust
use ratatui::layout::{Constraint, Flex, Layout, Rect};

fn centered_rect(area: Rect, width: Constraint, height: Constraint) -> Rect {
    let [area] = Layout::vertical([height]).flex(Flex::Center).areas(area);
    let [area] = Layout::horizontal([width]).flex(Flex::Center).areas(area);
    area
}

let popup_area = centered_rect(frame.area(), Constraint::Percentage(60), Constraint::Percentage(40));
```

## Rendering the popup over existing content

The key trick is: draw your normal UI first, *then* draw the popup widget on top of it in
the same frame, using `Clear` to wipe whatever was underneath so the popup doesn't show
"ghost" characters bleeding through its background:

```rust
use ratatui::widgets::{Block, Borders, Clear, Paragraph};

frame.render_widget(&app_ui, frame.area()); // your normal UI, drawn first

if app.show_help_popup {
    let popup_area = frame.area().centered(Constraint::Percentage(50), Constraint::Percentage(40));

    frame.render_widget(Clear, popup_area); // blank out the area first
    let popup = Paragraph::new("Press q to close this help popup.")
        .block(Block::bordered().title(" Help "));
    frame.render_widget(popup, popup_area);
}
```

`Clear` is a special zero-content widget whose only job is to reset every cell in its area to
blank/default before you draw over it — without it, characters from the widget underneath
can visually poke through gaps (e.g. between a bordered box's border and its content) because
ratatui only overwrites cells that the new widget actually writes to.

## Popups with a shadow

For a drop-shadow effect behind a popup (purely cosmetic, offsetting a dim block behind and
to the side of the popup rect), see the built-in `Shadow` widget example in the widget
reference — it's a small additional `Rect` offset by 1 cell, rendered before the popup itself
with a dim/gray background.

Layout.Scrolling Content:
# Scrolling Content

Ratatui widgets don't scroll automatically — a `Paragraph` or `List` bigger than its area
just gets clipped. Scrolling means tracking an offset yourself and passing it to the widget
each frame, then optionally rendering a `Scrollbar` alongside it to show position.

## Scrolling a Paragraph

```rust
use ratatui::widgets::Paragraph;

let paragraph = Paragraph::new(long_text)
    .scroll((app.scroll_offset, 0)); // (vertical offset, horizontal offset), in lines/cols
```

Update `app.scroll_offset` in your input handler (e.g. on Up/Down or PageUp/PageDown),
clamping it so it can't scroll past the content's total line count.

## Scrolling a List (built-in selection + scroll)

`List` handles this for you via `ListState` — see [List](/ratatui/list-widget/) for the full
selection API. Selecting an item automatically keeps it visible/in view.

## Adding a Scrollbar

`Scrollbar` is a thin visual indicator you render alongside your scrollable content; you
still own the actual scroll position via `ScrollbarState`:

```rust
use ratatui::widgets::{Scrollbar, ScrollbarOrientation, ScrollbarState};

struct App {
    scroll: usize,
    scrollbar_state: ScrollbarState,
}

impl App {
    fn set_content_length(&mut self, len: usize) {
        self.scrollbar_state = self.scrollbar_state.content_length(len);
    }

    fn scroll_down(&mut self) {
        self.scroll = self.scroll.saturating_add(1);
        self.scrollbar_state = self.scrollbar_state.position(self.scroll);
    }
}
```

Rendering it, typically along the right edge of a content area:

```rust
let scrollbar = Scrollbar::new(ScrollbarOrientation::VerticalRight)
    .begin_symbol(Some("↑"))
    .end_symbol(Some("↓"));

frame.render_stateful_widget(scrollbar, area, &mut app.scrollbar_state);
```

As of 0.30, you can read the current position back out of the state with
`ScrollbarState::get_position()`, which is handy if something else needs to react to where
the user has scrolled to (e.g. lazy-loading more content near the bottom).

## Clamping scroll offsets

Always clamp your scroll offset against the actual content height and viewport height so you
can't scroll past the end:

```rust
let max_scroll = content_line_count.saturating_sub(viewport_height as usize);
app.scroll = app.scroll.min(max_scroll);
```

Widgets.Paragraph:
# Paragraph

`Paragraph` renders (optionally wrapped, optionally scrolled) text, and is the widget you
reach for most often for any block of prose, logs, or formatted output.

## Basic usage

```rust
use ratatui::widgets::Paragraph;

let paragraph = Paragraph::new("Some text that might be quite long.");
frame.render_widget(paragraph, area);
```

## From styled Text/Lines

```rust
use ratatui::text::{Line, Text};
use ratatui::style::Stylize;

let text = Text::from(vec![
    Line::from("Title").bold(),
    Line::from(vec!["Status: ".into(), "OK".green()]),
]);
let paragraph = Paragraph::new(text);
```

## Wrapping

By default, text that's too wide for the area is clipped, not wrapped. Enable wrapping
explicitly:

```rust
use ratatui::widgets::Wrap;

let paragraph = Paragraph::new(long_text).wrap(Wrap { trim: true });
```

`trim: true` trims leading whitespace on wrapped lines; `trim: false` preserves it (useful
for pre-formatted/indented text like code blocks).

## Alignment

```rust
use ratatui::layout::HorizontalAlignment;

let paragraph = Paragraph::new("centered").alignment(HorizontalAlignment::Center);
// or shorthand:
let paragraph = Paragraph::new("centered").centered();
```

## Scrolling

```rust
let paragraph = Paragraph::new(long_text).scroll((app.scroll_y, app.scroll_x));
```

See [Scrolling Content](/ratatui/scrolling-content/) for pairing this with a `Scrollbar`.

## With a surrounding Block

```rust
use ratatui::widgets::{Block, Paragraph};

let paragraph = Paragraph::new("body text")
    .block(Block::bordered().title("Details"))
    .wrap(ratatui::widgets::Wrap { trim: true });

frame.render_widget(paragraph, area);
```

## Styling

```rust
use ratatui::style::{Color, Style};

let paragraph = Paragraph::new("text")
    .style(Style::default().fg(Color::White).bg(Color::Black));
```

Individual `Span`s inside the paragraph's `Text` can still carry their own styles on top of
this base style — see [Text, Spans, and Lines](/ratatui/text-spans-and-lines/).

Widgets.List:
# List

`List` renders a vertical list of items with optional single-item selection/highlighting. It
is a **stateful widget** — selection state lives in a `ListState` you own on your `App`.

## Basic usage

```rust
use ratatui::widgets::{List, ListState};

struct App {
    items: Vec<String>,
    list_state: ListState,
}

let list = List::new(app.items.clone())
    .block(ratatui::widgets::Block::bordered().title("Items"));

frame.render_stateful_widget(list, area, &mut app.list_state);
```

## Selection and highlighting

```rust
use ratatui::style::{Color, Modifier, Style};

let list = List::new(app.items.clone())
    .highlight_style(Style::default().bg(Color::Blue).add_modifier(Modifier::BOLD))
    .highlight_symbol("> ");
```

As of 0.30, `highlight_symbol` accepts anything convertible into a `Line`, so the symbol
itself can carry its own style independent of `highlight_style`:

```rust
use ratatui::text::Line;
use ratatui::style::Stylize;

let list = List::new(app.items.clone())
    .highlight_symbol(Line::from(">>").red().bold());
```

## Moving the selection

```rust
impl App {
    fn select_next(&mut self) {
        let i = match self.list_state.selected() {
            Some(i) if i + 1 < self.items.len() => i + 1,
            Some(i) => i, // already at the end, stay put
            None => 0,
        };
        self.list_state.select(Some(i));
    }

    fn select_previous(&mut self) {
        let i = match self.list_state.selected() {
            Some(0) | None => 0,
            Some(i) => i - 1,
        };
        self.list_state.select(Some(i));
    }
}
```

`ListState` automatically scrolls the visible window so the selected item stays on-screen —
you don't need to compute that yourself.

## Multi-select

`List` itself only tracks a single selected index. For multi-select, keep your own
`HashSet<usize>` of selected indices on `App`, render the list normally (using `ListState`
only for the "focused"/cursor item), and apply your own styling per-item based on membership
in that set:

```rust
use ratatui::widgets::ListItem;
use ratatui::style::Stylize;

let items: Vec<ListItem> = app.items.iter().enumerate().map(|(i, text)| {
    if app.selected_indices.contains(&i) {
        ListItem::new(text.as_str()).style(ratatui::style::Style::new().green())
    } else {
        ListItem::new(text.as_str())
    }
}).collect();

let list = List::new(items);
```

## Direction

```rust
use ratatui::widgets::ListDirection;

let list = List::new(app.items.clone()).direction(ListDirection::BottomToTop); // like a chat log
```

Widgets.Table:
# Table

`Table` renders rows and columns with optional headers and row selection. Like `List`, it's
a **stateful widget** using `TableState`.

## Basic usage

```rust
use ratatui::widgets::{Table, Row, Cell, TableState};
use ratatui::layout::Constraint;

let header = Row::new(vec!["Name", "Status", "Score"]);
let rows = vec![
    Row::new(vec!["Alice", "Active", "98"]),
    Row::new(vec!["Bob", "Idle", "76"]),
];

let table = Table::new(rows, [
        Constraint::Length(12),
        Constraint::Length(10),
        Constraint::Length(6),
    ])
    .header(header)
    .block(ratatui::widgets::Block::bordered().title("Players"));

frame.render_stateful_widget(table, area, &mut app.table_state); // app.table_state: TableState
```

The array/slice of `Constraint`s passed to `Table::new` sets each column's width, using the
same constraint kinds as [Layout Basics](/ratatui/layout-basics/) (`Length`, `Min`,
`Percentage`, `Fill`, etc).

## Styling rows and cells

```rust
use ratatui::style::{Color, Style, Stylize};
use ratatui::widgets::{Row, Cell};

let row = Row::new(vec![
    Cell::from("Alice"),
    Cell::from("Active").style(Style::new().green()),
    Cell::from("98"),
]).style(Style::default().bg(Color::Black));
```

## Row selection and highlighting

```rust
use ratatui::style::{Color, Modifier, Style};

let table = Table::new(rows, widths)
    .row_highlight_style(Style::default().bg(Color::Blue).add_modifier(Modifier::BOLD))
    .highlight_symbol(">> ");

impl App {
    fn select_next_row(&mut self) {
        let i = self.table_state.selected().map_or(0, |i| (i + 1).min(self.rows.len() - 1));
        self.table_state.select(Some(i));
    }
}
```

## Column spacing and header styling

```rust
let table = Table::new(rows, widths)
    .header(
        Row::new(vec!["Name", "Status", "Score"])
            .style(Style::default().add_modifier(Modifier::BOLD)),
    )
    .column_spacing(2);
```

## Row heights (multi-line rows)

```rust
let row = Row::new(vec!["Alice", "Multi\nline\ncell"]).height(3);
```

Widgets.Tabs:
# Tabs

`Tabs` renders a horizontal row of labeled tabs, typically used to switch between top-level
screens of an app.

## Basic usage

```rust
use ratatui::widgets::Tabs;

let tabs = Tabs::new(vec!["Home", "Logs", "Settings"])
    .select(app.selected_tab) // usize index of the active tab
    .block(ratatui::widgets::Block::bordered());

frame.render_widget(tabs, area);
```

## Styling

```rust
use ratatui::style::{Color, Modifier, Style};

let tabs = Tabs::new(vec!["Home", "Logs", "Settings"])
    .select(app.selected_tab)
    .style(Style::default().fg(Color::White))
    .highlight_style(Style::default().fg(Color::Yellow).add_modifier(Modifier::BOLD));
```

## Custom divider

```rust
let tabs = Tabs::new(vec!["Home", "Logs", "Settings"]).divider(" | ");
```

## Measuring total width (0.30+)

`Tabs::width()` computes the total rendered width including dividers and padding — useful if
you need to know ahead of time whether all tabs fit in the available area, or want to
right-align something else next to the tab bar:

```rust
let tabs = Tabs::new(vec!["Home", "Logs", "Settings"]);
let total_width = tabs.width();
```

## Wiring up tab switching

```rust
impl App {
    fn next_tab(&mut self) {
        self.selected_tab = (self.selected_tab + 1) % self.tab_titles.len();
    }

    fn previous_tab(&mut self) {
        self.selected_tab = self.selected_tab
            .checked_sub(1)
            .unwrap_or(self.tab_titles.len() - 1);
    }
}
```

Then render whichever screen/component corresponds to `app.selected_tab` in the remaining
area below the `Tabs` widget — see [Application Architecture](/ratatui/application-architecture/)
for structuring multiple tab-screens as separate components.

Widgets.BarChart and Sparkline:
# BarChart and Sparkline

Two widgets for visualizing numeric series: `BarChart` for labeled discrete values,
`Sparkline` for a compact continuous trend line.

## BarChart

```rust
use ratatui::widgets::BarChart;

let data = [("Mon", 12), ("Tue", 18), ("Wed", 7), ("Thu", 22), ("Fri", 15)];
let chart = BarChart::default()
    .block(ratatui::widgets::Block::bordered().title("Requests / day"))
    .data(&data)
    .bar_width(6)
    .bar_gap(1);

frame.render_widget(chart, area);
```

### The 0.30 constructor shorthands

New constructors avoid the builder pattern for the common case of building a chart straight
from data, including grouped bar charts (multiple bars sharing a category label):

```rust
use ratatui::widgets::{BarChart, BarGroup, Bar};

let chart = BarChart::grouped(vec![
    BarGroup::with_label("Mon", vec![Bar::with_label("Sent", 10), Bar::with_label("Recv", 6)]),
    BarGroup::with_label("Tue", vec![Bar::with_label("Sent", 14), Bar::with_label("Recv", 9)]),
]);
```

`BarChart::vertical(...)` and `BarChart::horizontal(...)` construct single-orientation charts
directly from a list of `Bar`s without needing `BarGroup` at all.

### Per-bar styling

```rust
use ratatui::widgets::Bar;
use ratatui::style::Stylize;

let bar = Bar::with_label("Errors", 3).style(ratatui::style::Style::new().red());
```

(`Bar` implements `Styled` as of 0.30, so `.red()`/`.on_black()`-style shorthand methods work
directly on it too.)

## Sparkline

A single-line, axis-less trend indicator — good for compact "last N values" displays in a
dashboard header or status bar:

```rust
use ratatui::widgets::Sparkline;
use ratatui::style::{Color, Style};

let data = [4, 8, 5, 9, 2, 7, 6, 10, 3];
let sparkline = Sparkline::default()
    .block(ratatui::widgets::Block::bordered().title("CPU %"))
    .data(&data)
    .style(Style::default().fg(Color::Green));

frame.render_widget(sparkline, area);
```

Keep a `VecDeque<u64>` of recent samples on your `App`, pushing new values and popping old
ones on each tick, to drive a live-updating sparkline (see
[Animations & Effects](/ratatui/animations-and-effects/) for the tick-driven update pattern).

Widgets.Calendar and Scrollbar:
# Calendar and Scrollbar

## Calendar

The `widgets::calendar` module renders a month grid and requires the `time` crate for date
handling:

```bash
cargo add ratatui --features calendar
cargo add time
```

```rust
use ratatui::widgets::calendar::{CalendarEventStore, Monthly};
use ratatui::style::{Color, Style};
use time::{Date, Month};

let date = Date::from_calendar_date(2026, Month::September, 1)?;
let events = CalendarEventStore::default(); // populate with .add(date, style) per event

let calendar = Monthly::new(date, events)
    .show_month_header(Style::default().fg(Color::Cyan))
    .default_style(Style::default());

frame.render_widget(calendar, area);
```

Mark specific dates (e.g. events, deadlines, "today") by inserting styled entries into the
`CalendarEventStore`:

```rust
let mut events = CalendarEventStore::default();
events.add(
    Date::from_calendar_date(2026, Month::September, 17)?,
    Style::default().bg(Color::Yellow).fg(Color::Black),
);
```

## Scrollbar

Covered in depth in [Scrolling Content](/ratatui/scrolling-content/) — quick reference:

```rust
use ratatui::widgets::{Scrollbar, ScrollbarOrientation, ScrollbarState};

let mut state = ScrollbarState::new(total_items).position(current_position);

let scrollbar = Scrollbar::new(ScrollbarOrientation::VerticalRight);
frame.render_stateful_widget(scrollbar, area, &mut state);
```

`ScrollbarOrientation` also offers `VerticalLeft`, `HorizontalTop`, and `HorizontalBottom` for
scrollbars on any edge of a widget.

Widgets.Building Custom Widgets:
# Building Custom Widgets

When none of the built-in widgets fit, implement the `Widget` trait yourself. This is the
same trait every built-in widget implements, so custom widgets are first-class — they render
with the exact same `frame.render_widget(...)` call.

## Minimal stateless widget

```rust
use ratatui::buffer::Buffer;
use ratatui::layout::Rect;
use ratatui::style::{Color, Style};
use ratatui::widgets::Widget;

struct ColorSwatch {
    color: Color,
}

impl Widget for ColorSwatch {
    fn render(self, area: Rect, buf: &mut Buffer) {
        for y in area.top()..area.bottom() {
            for x in area.left()..area.right() {
                buf[(x, y)].set_style(Style::default().bg(self.color));
                buf[(x, y)].set_symbol(" ");
            }
        }
    }
}

frame.render_widget(ColorSwatch { color: Color::Rgb(255, 100, 0) }, area);
```

Note `render(self, ...)` takes `self` by value, not `&self` — widgets are meant to be cheap,
throwaway builder values constructed fresh each frame, consistent with the rest of ratatui's
immediate-mode design (see [Rendering & the Frame Loop](/ratatui/rendering/)).

## Delegating to existing widgets

Often a "custom widget" is really just composing existing ones — implement `Widget` and call
`.render()` on inner widgets you construct:

```rust
use ratatui::widgets::{Block, Paragraph, Widget};

struct StatusPanel<'a> {
    message: &'a str,
    ok: bool,
}

impl<'a> Widget for StatusPanel<'a> {
    fn render(self, area: Rect, buf: &mut Buffer) {
        let color = if self.ok { Color::Green } else { Color::Red };
        let block = Block::bordered().title("Status");
        let inner = block.inner(area);
        block.render(area, buf);
        Paragraph::new(self.message)
            .style(Style::default().fg(color))
            .render(inner, buf);
    }
}
```

## Stateful custom widgets

Implement `StatefulWidget` instead of `Widget` when your widget needs to read/write some
external state during rendering (mirroring how `List`/`Table`/`Scrollbar` work):

```rust
use ratatui::widgets::StatefulWidget;

struct Counter;

#[derive(Default)]
struct CounterState {
    value: i64,
}

impl StatefulWidget for Counter {
    type State = CounterState;

    fn render(self, area: Rect, buf: &mut Buffer, state: &mut Self::State) {
        let text = format!("Count: {}", state.value);
        buf.set_string(area.x, area.y, text, Style::default());
    }
}

frame.render_stateful_widget(Counter, area, &mut app.counter_state);
```

## Directly writing into the Buffer

For anything not covered by helper methods, you can index the `Buffer` directly:

```rust
buf[(x, y)].set_symbol("█").set_style(Style::default().fg(Color::Red));
// or, for a whole string at once:
buf.set_string(x, y, "hello", Style::default());
```

Always bound your writes to the given `area` — writing outside it can corrupt whatever else
is sharing the buffer this frame (ratatui does not clip for you inside a custom `render`
implementation).

## Publishing a widget crate

If you're distributing your widget as its own crate for others to use, prefer depending on
`ratatui-core` rather than the full `ratatui` facade crate — `ratatui-core` evolves more
slowly and gives consumers of your widget a more stable API surface across ratatui point
releases. See [Choosing a Backend](/ratatui/choosing-a-backend/) for the full workspace
layout.

Rendering & Performance.Testing Ratatui Apps:
# Testing Ratatui Apps

Because rendering is a pure function of `(widget, area) -> Buffer` contents, ratatui apps are
unusually easy to unit test without a real terminal at all.

## TestBackend

`TestBackend` renders into an in-memory `Buffer` you can assert against directly, with no
real terminal I/O:

```rust
use ratatui::{Terminal, backend::TestBackend};

#[test]
fn renders_hello_world() {
    let backend = TestBackend::new(20, 3);
    let mut terminal = Terminal::new(backend).unwrap();

    terminal.draw(|frame| {
        frame.render_widget("Hello World!", frame.area());
    }).unwrap();

    let buffer = terminal.backend().buffer();
    assert_eq!(buffer.content()[0].symbol(), "H");
}
```

## Building an expected Buffer directly

For asserting on a whole rendered frame, construct the expected `Buffer` by hand and compare:

```rust
use ratatui::buffer::Buffer;
use ratatui::layout::Rect;

let expected = Buffer::with_lines(vec!["Hello World!       "]); // padded to full width
assert_eq!(terminal.backend().buffer(), &expected);
```

`Buffer`'s `Debug`/`PartialEq` implementations produce readable diffs on failure, showing
exactly which cells differ.

## Snapshot testing with insta

For anything beyond a couple of lines, hand-writing expected buffers gets unwieldy fast —
most real ratatui projects use [`insta`](https://crates.io/crates/insta) snapshot tests
instead, asserting against a stored `.snap` file that you review and approve on intentional
UI changes:

```bash
cargo add --dev insta
cargo install cargo-insta
```

```rust
#[test]
fn snapshot_of_main_screen() {
    let backend = TestBackend::new(80, 24);
    let mut terminal = Terminal::new(backend).unwrap();
    terminal.draw(|frame| ui::draw(&App::default(), frame)).unwrap();

    insta::assert_debug_snapshot!(terminal.backend().buffer());
}
```

Run `cargo insta review` after a UI change to interactively accept/reject the diff against
the previous snapshot.

## Testing custom widget state

For widgets carrying their own internal state (e.g. a custom stateful widget from
[Building Custom Widgets](/ratatui/custom-widgets/)), test the state-mutation logic directly
as plain Rust — since `update`/event-handling functions ideally don't touch rendering at all
(see [The Elm-Style App Loop](/ratatui/the-elm-style-app-loop/)), most of your app's actual
*logic* can be tested with zero ratatui involvement, leaving `TestBackend`/`insta` just for
the rendering layer itself.

Rendering & Performance.Animations & Effects:
# Animations & Effects

Ratatui itself has no animation system — it's a stateless rendering library that draws
exactly what you tell it to draw, once per `terminal.draw` call. "Animation" in a ratatui app
is just: advance some numeric state on a timer, and let your normal `draw` function render
whatever that state currently says.

## The tick-driven pattern

This is the same non-blocking-poll loop from
[The Elm-Style App Loop](/ratatui/the-elm-style-app-loop/), the foundation every ratatui
animation is built on:

```rust
use std::time::{Duration, Instant};
use crossterm::event::{self, Event};

let tick_rate = Duration::from_millis(50); // 20 ticks/sec
let mut last_tick = Instant::now();

loop {
    terminal.draw(|frame| app.draw(frame))?;

    let timeout = tick_rate.saturating_sub(last_tick.elapsed());
    if event::poll(timeout)? {
        if let Event::Key(key) = event::read()? {
            app.handle_key(key);
        }
    }

    if last_tick.elapsed() >= tick_rate {
        app.on_tick();
        last_tick = Instant::now();
    }
}
```

`app.on_tick()` is where all animation state advances — a spinner's frame index, a gauge's
progress ratio, a scrolling marquee's offset, particles in a Canvas simulation, and so on.

## Example: a spinner

```rust
const SPINNER_FRAMES: [&str; 4] = ["|", "/", "-", "\\"];

struct App {
    spinner_index: usize,
}

impl App {
    fn on_tick(&mut self) {
        self.spinner_index = (self.spinner_index + 1) % SPINNER_FRAMES.len();
    }

    fn draw(&self, frame: &mut ratatui::Frame) {
        frame.render_widget(SPINNER_FRAMES[self.spinner_index], frame.area());
    }
}
```

## Example: a pulsing color

Compute a color from elapsed time using a sine wave, so brightness oscillates smoothly rather
than jumping between fixed steps:

```rust
use std::time::Instant;
use ratatui::style::Color;

fn pulsing_color(start: Instant) -> Color {
    let t = start.elapsed().as_secs_f64();
    let brightness = ((t * 2.0).sin() * 0.5 + 0.5) * 255.0; // oscillates 0..255
    Color::Rgb(brightness as u8, 0, 0)
}
```

## Easing manually

For anything beyond linear interpolation (ease-in/out curves, spring physics), you're
writing plain Rust math against your own progress value — there's nothing ratatui-specific
here, just standard tweening formulas applied to whatever numeric field you're animating,
evaluated fresh in `draw` each frame based on elapsed time.

## tachyonfx — a dedicated effects/animation crate

For anything more elaborate than manual tick math — fades, wipes, glitches, shatter effects,
color transitions, composed/layered/sequenced effects — the ecosystem crate
[`tachyonfx`](https://github.com/ratatui/tachyonfx) is purpose-built for ratatui and is the
standard answer for "how do I make this transition smooth" in the ratatui community:

```bash
cargo add tachyonfx
```

It lets you build complex animations by composing small effect primitives and layering or
sequencing them, driven by the same tick/elapsed-time model described above rather than
anything ratatui needs special support for. See the
[tachyonfx documentation](https://docs.rs/tachyonfx/latest/tachyonfx/fx/index.html) and its
[examples](https://github.com/ratatui/tachyonfx/tree/development/examples) for the full
effects API, or try effects interactively in the browser via the
[Tachyonfx FTL](https://junkdog.github.io/tachyonfx-ftl/) playground (itself a ratatui app
compiled to WASM via Ratzilla).

## Frame rate considerations

Terminal redraws are much cheaper than typical GUI redraws (see
[Rendering & the Frame Loop](/ratatui/rendering/) on the double-buffer diff), but there's
still no reason to tick faster than the eye needs — 20-60 ticks/second is the normal range
for smooth-looking terminal animation; going much higher mostly burns CPU without visible
benefit, especially over SSH where higher tick rates also mean more escape-code traffic.
